      "ai_overview_text": "In the UK, a business leased line typically costs between \u00a3130 and \u00a3700 per month (excluding VAT) for standard speeds. However, exceptional entry-level rates can drop as low as \u00a369 per month in highly competitive urban areas. Because leased lines are dedicated, symmetrical, uncontended connections backed by strict financial SLAs, the pricing is highly dependent on your exact postcode and bandwidth requirements. Average Leased Line Prices by Speed The following table outlines ballpark monthly rental costs for a typical 36-month contract: Key Factors Affecting Your Quote Location (The Biggest Factor): If your building is in an urban center (like London or Manchester) already passed by multiple networks (e.g., Openreach, Virgin Media Business, CityFibre), providers will compete heavily, lowering your cost. If you are in a rural or isolated area, monthly fees will skew much higher because the provider must recoup the infrastructure costs. Contract Length: The standard commitment is 36 to 60 months. Opting for a longer contract almost always lowers your monthly premium and usually waives upfront costs. Bearer Size vs. Speed: You can buy a \"100Mbps on a 1Gbps bearer.\" This means you get 100Mbps speeds today, but you can instantly upgrade your speed to 1Gbps later without needing new physical lines dug up. Setup and Hidden Costs Standard Installation: Typically runs between \u00a3500 and \u00a32,000 as a one-off fee, but it is frequently waived completely if you sign a 3-year or 5-year contract. Excess Construction Charges (ECCs): If a provider has to dig up the road or install extensive new physical fiber to reach your building, they may issue an ECC. Major networks like BT Business often cover the first chunk of ECCs (e.g., up to \u00a32,800), but if the work is highly complex, you could face unexpected upfront fees. You always have the right to cancel the contract without penalty if the survey reveals ECCs you don't want to pay. Next Step: Because quotes are structured on a building-b",
